  "source96": {
    "derivation": "If g(0)=0 and g differentiable at0 with derivative a, then g(s\u00b2)=a*s\u00b2+o(s\u00b2), H(t)=a*t\u00b3/3+o(t\u00b3), F(x)=a*(x^8-x^12)/12+o(x^8), so sin(F(x))/x^8 -> a/12. The given1/12 determines a=1."
  },
  "source62": {
    "caveat": "Converter claims infinity forces center pi, which is false for an exterior Laurent annulus centered elsewhere. For c=0, sin(z)/(z-pi)^2 has an exterior Laurent expansion for |z|>pi with outer radius infinity. Center pi is plausible but not unique. Do not assert unique identification."
  },
  "source87": {
    "caveat": "The spike counterexample satisfies f(x)/x^p ->0 for every p<3, so exponent2 is not uniquely identified. Open-ended ambiguity is allowed; do not assert it must be2."
